You already found the pod running your Spring Boot service:

springboot-deployment-fa-86749775ff-758rw

Now to see logs for this Spring Boot service, use the kubectl logs command.


---

1️⃣ View Logs of Spring Boot Pod

kubectl logs springboot-deployment-fa-86749775ff-758rw -n cbops --kubeconfig h06vksuatcbopscls.conf

This will print the complete logs of the Spring Boot application.


---

2️⃣ View Live Logs (Most Useful)

Developers usually watch logs in real time:

kubectl logs -f springboot-deployment-fa-86749775ff-758rw -n cbops --kubeconfig h06vksuatcbopscls.conf

-f = follow logs (live streaming)

You will see logs like:

Starting SpringBootApplication
Tomcat started on port 8080
Connected to database
Request received /login


---

3️⃣ If Pod Has Multiple Containers

Check containers first:

kubectl describe pod springboot-deployment-fa-86749775ff-758rw -n cbops --kubeconfig h06vksuatcbopscls.conf

Then use:

kubectl logs springboot-deployment-fa-86749775ff-758rw -c container-name -n cbops --kubeconfig h06vksuatcbopscls.conf


---

4️⃣ Show Only Recent Logs (Very Useful)

kubectl logs --tail=100 springboot-deployment-fa-86749775ff-758rw -n cbops --kubeconfig h06vksuatcbopscls.conf

Shows last 100 logs.


---

5️⃣ Logs Since Last 10 Minutes

kubectl logs --since=10m springboot-deployment-fa-86749775ff-758rw -n cbops --kubeconfig h06vksuatcbopscls.conf


---

6️⃣ Pro Developer Trick (Filter Errors)

kubectl logs -f springboot-deployment-fa-86749775ff-758rw -n cbops --kubeconfig h06vksuatcbopscls.conf | grep ERROR

Shows only error logs.


---

✅ Best command for your case

kubectl logs -f springboot-deployment-fa-86749775ff-758rw -n cbops --kubeconfig h06vksuatcbopscls.conf


